id #edb/ref 17592186438349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d4a1be4-1771-5700-ad05-4ae3a028b5b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438349} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b5b96a8-998c-5f10-bb84-99eac175deab" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f506060c-db8a-5e26-aad7-74b6b6b44f61" :question/math-academy-id "q-48068" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a3874b7907227653d93451d9ce0960fdadc8cb3e10396e0f27f7ebb363bebe2.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite\n- **II.** ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ is **not** finite\n- **III.** $f(x)$  is  continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function doesn't have a break at $x=-1$ and we can draw the graph of $f(x)$ around $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176053 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92d6129b-a5c5-597a-86ee-3f19af20f326" :knowledge-point/title "Describing the General Properties of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e211abea-7b27-5a54-be2b-2776b824b945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438350} {:answer/id #uuid "79899ac4-ccf6-540e-b658-cb8b94ac43da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175